{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's mechanism conflates identical observable error signatures with a shared causal trigger, but this is not a reliable evidentiary discriminator. The 'Telemetry Gap' countercondition only covers missing logs; the deeper fault is that the same error signature can arise from fully independent causes (two independent bugs producing the same parse failure in different deployments of the same library), while a shared cause can produce diverse signatures (a common upstream outage causing one node to timeout, another to OOM). Thus 'Error Signature Diversity' is neither necessary nor sufficient for independence, so it does not distinguish truly independent from merely apparently uncorrelated failures as the problem demands.","counterexample":["Two nodes independently hit the same memory-corruption bug in a shared library and produce byte-identical stack traces; Error Signature Diversity is zero but the failures are independently triggered, not caused by a shared trigger."],"target":"9640106ed83a39a566ec4e708c6de7d8239f47b297748c2085f73b4a67597ccf"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues the large service wins by positing two separate mechanisms (centralized patching and coordination complexity), but the patch-latency claim has no demonstrated causal link to correlated failure. Unpatched nodes being 'weakest links' that 'cascade' through API calls actually shows correlated failure spreading in the decentralized network, yet the argument never addresses the symmetric mechanism for the large service: a single logic bug in core code is replicated across every redundant node, so the patch reaches all nodes instantly but the failure reaches all nodes instantly too. By assuming the large service's redundancy is not all running identical code, the argument suppresses the very correlated failure mode that makes replicated redundancy fragile, so the causal mechanism is incomplete relative to the problem.","target":"b18c4d5c9913cee3ef190d2b528ab2fb766d12369223039d2a4bdb47c1dea74d"}]}